#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465 :topic/id #uuid "4bbf23bd-b30a-5ef9-8f1c-5c3855237fe0" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068637 :question/id #uuid "3ecf6cf7-468d-53d9-b52f-6aa69ce45306" :question/math-academy-id "e-7054" :question/problem "Solving the equation $25x^{2}-20x+4= 0$ using the quadratic formula leads to the expression below:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{(-20)^2-4\\cdot  25 \\cdot  \\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}}}} {2 \\cdot 25}\n$$\nWhat number is missing from the formula, and what is the solution of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na=25, \\quad b=-20, \\quad c=4\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-20) ± \\sqrt{(-20)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (4)})/(2 \\cdot 25) \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{20^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot \\color{blue}\\,4\\,}}{2 \\cdot 25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\boxed{ { {\\color{blue}\\hspace{0.167em} 4 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}.$\n\nNow, to find the solution, we simplify the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{20^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot \\color{blue}\\,4\\,}}{2 \\cdot 25} &= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{400 - 400}}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{0}}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± 0}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93dea6c-b664-523e-a52f-985792115895" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quadratic Formula Using a Framework"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068862 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068500 :question/id #uuid "fe2f52aa-f883-5455-8d35-94ddc6a5cb1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1434" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $2x^2 - 3x - 5 = 0$." :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 2, \\quad b = -3, \\quad c = -5\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(2)(-5)}}{2(2)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{49}}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3 \\pm 7}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{3 + 7}{4} = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 - 7}{4} =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "773cb449-9efe-5c0c-95ca-e433d8557efc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Quadratic Formula to Solve a Quadratic Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068501 :question/id #uuid "e1662bab-c11d-5770-8b7d-6646902f7e20" :question/math-academy-id "e-1435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $x^2 + 2x = 6$." :question/worked-solution "The first step is to put the equation into standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x &= 6 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x - 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\quad b = 2, \\quad c = -6\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(2) \\pm \\sqrt{(2)^2 - 4(1)(-6)}}{2(1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{28}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= -1 \\pm \\sqrt{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = -1 + \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx = -1 - \\sqrt{7}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c011eb9-7c5a-5180-812a-e6bbfcc7d93f" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying and Solving a Quadratic Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068502 :question/id #uuid "9812207c-cb3b-5522-a08a-c0bf4672a22e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1436" :question/problem "What are the solutions to $3x^2 = x - 10$?" :question/worked-solution "The first step is to put the equation into standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - x + 10 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients are\n\n$$\na = 3, \\quad b = -1, \\quad c = 10\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-1) ± \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} - 4(3)(10)})/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 ± \\sqrt{1 - 120}}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 ± \\sqrt{-119}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the equation has no real solutions because $\\sqrt{-119}$ is not a real number."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf857de-6a58-5450-9fab-2795f1317e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quadratic Formula To an Equation With No Real Solutions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 422 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334}] :topic/title "The Quadratic Formula"}]}
